  <h2 id="integrated-science">Integrated BS-MS: the research route through IISER and NISER</h2>

  <p>
    If you genuinely enjoy science for its own sake, not just for the exam, the 5-year integrated BS-MS at the IISERs or NISER is one of the most overlooked options on this entire list.
  </p>

  <p>
    You get in through the <strong>IISER Aptitude Test (IAT)</strong>, which needs roughly 60% aggregate across Class 12 subjects (55% for SC/ST/PwD candidates) and at least three of Biology, Chemistry, Maths, and Physics — maths becomes compulsory only for the computational, data-science, and economics-linked tracks.
    NEST, a separate exam, leads into NISER Bhubaneswar and the CEBS programme.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Scalable-leverage note:</strong> a BS-MS graduate who adds data or computational skills on top of core science depth often has a stronger, more flexible base than a narrow single-subject BSc — the integration is the advantage, not just the IISER brand name.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="niche-routes">Actuarial science, Merchant Navy, pilot training, and NDA</h2>

  <p>
    These four routes rarely appear in a standard "courses after 12th" list, but each one is a real, structured career with its own entrance gate.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Finance-adjacent</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">Actuarial science</div>
      <p>
        The Actuarial Common Entrance Test (ACET) is open to anyone who has appeared for or completed Class 12, with a genuine strength in maths and numbers as the real filter. Clearing ACET makes you a student member of the Institute of Actuaries of India, the first real step toward an actuary's qualification, usually alongside a BSc or BCom.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Maritime</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">Merchant Navy</div>
      <p>
        IMU CET requires at least 60% marks in PCM together at Class 12 and 50% in English, with age limits that vary by category. It opens BSc Nautical Science, BTech Marine Engineering, BTech Naval Architecture, and related officer-track courses.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Aviation</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">Pilot training (CPL)</div>
      <p>
        There is no single national entrance exam. Instead, you need a DGCA Class 1 medical clearance, minimum maths and physics marks at Class 12, and admission to a DGCA-approved flying school, usually alongside a BSc Aviation. This is one of the highest upfront-cost routes on this list, so verify the current total cost and financing options directly with the flying school before committing.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Defence</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">NDA</div>
      <p>
        The NDA written exam, run by UPSC, is followed by SSB interviews and medical tests. PCM at Class 12 is specifically required for the Air Force and Naval Academy wings, while the Army wing accepts any stream.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

